1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,280
They were the must-have tech treasures of their day that propelled us into modern life.

2
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:15,220
They entertained and organized, granted access, captured memories, and got us from A to B in

3
00:00:15,221 --> 00:00:20,860
cutting-edge style. Until we traded them in for the latest and greatest.

4
00:00:22,220 --> 00:00:23,980
Technology is moving very quickly.

5
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:26,280
But some are still worth a spin.

6
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:27,800
They still make records.

7
00:00:27,801 --> 00:00:31,800
Just don't get too attached, because what's new will soon

8
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:32,800
be old.

9
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,800
They still make CDs?

10
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:37,800
Now, Retro Tech on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,800
Not that long ago, these relics were parts that lived inside

12
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,800
our favorite gadgets.

13
00:00:56,801 --> 00:00:59,640
The ones we absolutely couldn't live without.

14
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,380
The ones we reluctantly retired to attics and garages.

15
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:10,100
These seemingly primitive products were once our connection to the world.

16
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:11,800
And to each other.

17
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,160
And no matter what new innovations we embrace,

18
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:19,320
we'll always recall the retro originals with nostalgic fondness.

19
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:25,240
Remember that cumbersome cathode ray tube TV set and its annoying antenna?

20
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:32,240
Since the 1940s, this icon was your ticket to a world of news and entertainment.

21
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:35,760
We didn't pay for anything. Advertising paid for everything.

22
00:01:36,200 --> 00:01:39,640
You didn't have a cable box. You didn't have wires connecting it.

23
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:40,980
You didn't have a satellite dish.

24
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:46,760
To get that free TV signal, you either had an aerial roof antenna

25
00:01:46,761 --> 00:01:49,780
or a pair of rabbit ears sitting atop the set.

26
00:01:49,781 --> 00:01:59,260
the CRT TV owes its size and heft to the large tube and the lead-laden glass screen

27
00:01:59,261 --> 00:02:02,960
that protects the viewer from the harmful x-rays generated inside

28
00:02:02,961 --> 00:02:13,060
in a CRT the cathode is a heated filament it sits in a vacuum inside a glass tube the ray is a

29
00:02:13,061 --> 00:02:18,260
stream of electrons that pours off the cathode becomes focused into a beam and strikes the

30
00:02:18,261 --> 00:02:23,400
phosphor-coated back of the screen, making it glow.

31
00:02:23,401 --> 00:02:27,400
A magnet guides the beam as it sweeps 525 horizontal lines

32
00:02:27,401 --> 00:02:30,860
across the screen, odd-numbered lines first,

33
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:34,660
then the even, which makes up a video frame.

34
00:02:34,661 --> 00:02:39,700
This happens 30 times every second to create the picture.

35
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:42,020
This is a standard version of a CRT TV.

36
00:02:42,021 --> 00:02:44,480
It's a semi-fish bowl on the front of the screen.

37
00:02:44,481 --> 00:02:47,860
So it is a little bit more reflective as far as light goes.

38
00:02:47,861 --> 00:02:50,480
It's a little bit bigger on the back, it's a little bit heavier,

39
00:02:50,481 --> 00:02:53,600
and it's going to take up a little bit more space on your counter or shelf.

40
00:02:54,220 --> 00:02:55,800
Did he say a little heavier?

41
00:02:56,420 --> 00:03:01,660
TVs back then, I would say at least 150 to 200 pounds to lift,

42
00:03:01,740 --> 00:03:04,380
so you need two strapping nails to lift that for you.

43
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:08,100
Now an old lady can lift one of the flat screen TVs.

44
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:12,020
40 or 42-inch screens weigh about 35, 40 pounds.

45
00:03:14,140 --> 00:03:16,380
The original television signal was analog,

46
00:03:16,381 --> 00:03:18,000
and it looked decent enough

47
00:03:18,001 --> 00:03:20,440
because frankly we didn't know any better.

48
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:24,460
Today that analog signal isn't just obsolete.

49
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:26,660
It's actually on the extinct list

50
00:03:26,661 --> 00:03:28,720
thanks to the digital revolution

51
00:03:28,721 --> 00:03:31,220
powered by the binary code of ones and zeros.

52
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,780
An analog signal is a wave.

53
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:39,060
Think of it as an up and down and up and down wave

54
00:03:39,061 --> 00:03:41,060
that's coming to you versus ones and zeros.

55
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:45,100
The benefit of digital is we can continually increase

56
00:03:45,101 --> 00:03:50,580
how tight we pack those zeros and ones to get increasing high fidelity.

57
00:03:50,820 --> 00:03:57,060
In other words, high-definition television today will be considered in 10 years low definition.

58
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:05,100
Digital TV broadcasts began in the late 1990s, and a decade later, analog's fate was sealed.

59
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:10,640
These are the flat panel beauties that line the walls of today's electronic stores.

60
00:04:10,641 --> 00:04:15,640
We have plasma screen TVs, we have LCD screen TVs.

61
00:04:15,641 --> 00:04:19,640
They're all high definition, and of course, we're showing a beautiful high definition picture on these.

62
00:04:19,641 --> 00:04:27,640
LCD and plasma technologies rely on small, streamlined components to deliver that superior picture quality,

63
00:04:27,641 --> 00:04:31,640
allowing the boob tube to go tubeless.

64
00:04:31,641 --> 00:04:37,640
And they don't produce x-radiation, so no need for the heavy-duty glass.

65
00:04:37,641 --> 00:04:43,880
glass. But you don't have to part with your beloved rabbit ears and CRTs just yet.

66
00:04:43,881 --> 00:04:51,232
The converter boxes have come out to actually boost the signal of the rabbit ears.

67
00:04:51,233 --> 00:04:51,933
CRT TVs

68
00:04:51,934 --> 00:04:57,980
can be hooked up to cable or satellite. The digital revolution triggered the

69
00:04:57,981 --> 00:05:05,900
decline of many technologies, most notably the vinyl LP. From the 50s to the 70s,

70
00:05:05,901 --> 00:05:09,660
Perhaps no single possession was more treasured by American teenagers

71
00:05:09,661 --> 00:05:12,040
than their collection of vinyl records.

72
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:19,840
Records on turntables and in jukeboxes provided the soundtrack to our most cherished memories.

73
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:26,920
Perhaps you or your parents fell in love to the music being played on one of these.

74
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:33,440
First, eight-track tapes and then cassettes challenged vinyl's reign.

75
00:05:33,441 --> 00:05:39,460
But it was this digitally encoded piece of plastic that ultimately toppled the record's regime.

76
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,220
By the mid-'80s, the recording industry virtually stopped releasing on vinyl.

77
00:05:47,520 --> 00:05:53,080
Of course, if history teaches us anything, it's that what goes around often comes around.

78
00:05:56,140 --> 00:06:01,780
Since the turn of the 20th century, audiophiles have been grooving to the rich sounds of needle on record.

79
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,240
Rainbow Records in Canoga Park, California,

80
00:06:06,241 --> 00:06:09,880
has been pressing vinyl discs since 1955.

81
00:06:09,881 --> 00:06:12,620
At the time, we were probably making

82
00:06:12,621 --> 00:06:15,960
around 6,000 to 8,000 pieces a day.

83
00:06:15,961 --> 00:06:18,460
In the 60s, we got much larger,

84
00:06:18,461 --> 00:06:20,920
and we were in the neighborhood of

85
00:06:20,921 --> 00:06:23,860
30,000 to 40,000 records a day.

86
00:06:23,861 --> 00:06:28,400
Our heyday was in 77, when Elvis died.

87
00:06:28,401 --> 00:06:30,540
Within three days of Elvis' death,

88
00:06:30,541 --> 00:06:33,300
we were pressing 60,000 records a day,

89
00:06:33,301 --> 00:06:34,300
seven days a week.

90
00:06:34,301 --> 00:06:45,540
By the late 80s, Rainbow was back down to pressing 8 to 10,000 vinyl records per day.

91
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:50,420
But thanks to loyal listeners and avid collectors, records never completely went extinct.

92
00:06:50,421 --> 00:06:54,680
And today, something interesting is happening.

93
00:06:54,681 --> 00:07:00,200
We are presently pressing close to 25,000 a day, six days a week.

94
00:07:00,201 --> 00:07:02,100
There's definitely a resurgence in vinyl.

95
00:07:02,101 --> 00:07:09,100
Creating a vinyl record has always been part science, part mechanics, part craft.

96
00:07:09,101 --> 00:07:15,100
The modern method has its share of high-tech tweaks, but the basics have endured.

97
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:22,100
In a studio, music is recorded as sound waves onto magnetic tape or digital medium.

98
00:07:22,101 --> 00:07:28,100
Those sound patterns are fed into a computer-controlled lathe,

99
00:07:28,101 --> 00:07:33,560
computer-controlled lathe where a heated sapphire tip cuts a continuous spiral groove into the

100
00:07:33,561 --> 00:07:37,713
surface of a lacquer-covered aluminum disc.

101
00:07:37,714 --> 00:07:42,060
The groove is a mechanical translation of the

102
00:07:42,061 --> 00:07:45,953
frequency and amplitude of the sound waves.

103
00:07:45,954 --> 00:07:50,480
Low bass tones produce a wide groove, while higher

104
00:07:50,481 --> 00:07:56,300
frequencies are narrower. Next, the lacquer is sent to Rainbow for duplication.

105
00:07:56,301 --> 00:08:02,320
We're going to spray it with silver, then we're going to hang it into a tank and build

106
00:08:02,321 --> 00:08:05,440
a nickel surface on it.

107
00:08:05,441 --> 00:08:09,520
The silver coating makes the surface conductive to electricity, allowing a current to pass

108
00:08:09,521 --> 00:08:14,640
through, and the nickel to adhere to it through electroplating.

109
00:08:14,641 --> 00:08:19,640
Then we're going to separate the two pieces.

110
00:08:19,641 --> 00:08:23,920
And then you had a positive and now you have a negative.

111
00:08:23,921 --> 00:08:29,460
The A and B side negatives, called the master, are placed back in the electroplating tank

112
00:08:29,461 --> 00:08:33,360
to create positives called the mother.

113
00:08:33,361 --> 00:08:38,140
From the mother, up to 40 pairs of negative stampers, which will press the final records,

114
00:08:38,141 --> 00:08:43,680
can be made before quality degrades.

115
00:08:43,681 --> 00:08:46,680
Now science gives way to the mechanics and craft of record making.

116
00:08:46,681 --> 00:08:49,680
This is a record press.

117
00:08:49,681 --> 00:08:55,680
We have two labels, two stampers which have the information on it.

118
00:08:55,681 --> 00:09:01,680
We glue the stamper onto the mold, so you've got an A-side and a B-side.

119
00:09:01,681 --> 00:09:05,680
You've got an A-side and a B-side label.

120
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:09,680
Workers pour PVC pellets into the machine.

121
00:09:09,681 --> 00:09:15,680
Where they're melted and formed into a malleable puck, it will become the disc.

122
00:09:15,681 --> 00:09:20,680
The polyvinyl chloride or PVC is about 330 degrees.

123
00:09:20,681 --> 00:09:25,680
The press is about 330 degrees when it closes.

124
00:09:25,681 --> 00:09:30,680
Goes under 1,800 pounds of pressure.

125
00:09:30,681 --> 00:09:34,680
And it squeezes out, and it's kind of like making a waffle.

126
00:09:34,681 --> 00:09:39,680
Instead of pouring in batter, we're pouring in vinyl.

127
00:09:39,681 --> 00:09:44,680
About 30 seconds later, the formed disc emerges on the other side.

128
00:09:44,681 --> 00:09:48,680
After the record gets pressed and it gets cooled down, it

129
00:09:48,780 --> 00:09:52,680
comes out to the trimming station.

130
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:55,820
And the trimming station trims off the excess.

131
00:09:55,920 --> 00:10:00,020
This excess will recycle and use again once the record is

132
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:03,840
trimmed, that record is finished, other than quality

133
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:08,240
control and packaging.

134
00:10:08,340 --> 00:10:13,080
By contrast, Rainbow makes CDs, about 100,000 a day,

135
00:10:13,081 --> 00:10:15,880
in sterile-looking rooms on the other side of the factory.

136
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:21,240
Industry-wide, CD's market share has been dwindling,

137
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:23,920
thanks in large part to digital downloads

138
00:10:23,921 --> 00:10:26,420
and in small part to the resurgence of vinyl.

139
00:10:29,060 --> 00:10:31,700
But for some, the record was never superseded.

140
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:34,820
This is a digital-free zone.

141
00:10:36,040 --> 00:10:37,980
Welcome to the record collector,

142
00:10:38,700 --> 00:10:41,160
which I established in 1974

143
00:10:41,161 --> 00:10:44,420
with my personal collection of 50,078s,

144
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:47,480
another 450,000 LPs,

145
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:52,480
300,000 classical, 100,000 jazz,

146
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:53,960
and 100,000 other.

147
00:10:55,320 --> 00:10:58,640
And the records go 13 feet high.

148
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:02,900
Sandy is passionate about vinyl

149
00:11:02,901 --> 00:11:05,100
and not shy with his opinion of CDs.

150
00:11:06,300 --> 00:11:09,280
It loses a lot of the body and nuance

151
00:11:09,281 --> 00:11:10,960
to the original performance.

152
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:13,160
and authenticity is everything.

153
00:11:13,320 --> 00:11:15,460
And if there's no authenticity and no integrity,

154
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:19,480
and you're going to superimpose technical gadgetry

155
00:11:19,481 --> 00:11:23,840
as a good substitute for your musical experience,

156
00:11:23,980 --> 00:11:25,320
I think you're being had.

157
00:11:26,660 --> 00:11:29,020
Any record lover would be hard-pressed

158
00:11:29,021 --> 00:11:31,100
to find a richer trove of vinyl treasures.

159
00:11:32,220 --> 00:11:36,940
It is the first complete recording of Carmen by Bizet,

160
00:11:37,460 --> 00:11:41,020
recorded in 1913, issued in Berlin, Germany.

161
00:11:41,160 --> 00:11:43,500
on gramophone records.

162
00:11:43,960 --> 00:11:48,980
The Mozart 40th Symphony, recorded in 1926.

163
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:53,220
Bruno Walter conducting the Berlin State Opera Orchestra,

164
00:11:54,180 --> 00:11:55,240
recorded in Berlin.

165
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:03,300
It's not just the classics that live on vinyl.

166
00:12:04,100 --> 00:12:06,560
Today's big-selling artists are back on the turntable,

167
00:12:06,940 --> 00:12:09,620
and the kids are finding out what their parents already knew.

168
00:12:09,621 --> 00:12:14,620
There's audiophile guys who are still buying records,

169
00:12:14,621 --> 00:12:18,620
but what's really driving it today is 13 to 24-year-olds

170
00:12:18,621 --> 00:12:21,620
who have discovered this new thing called a record.

171
00:12:21,621 --> 00:12:23,620
People always ask you what you do for a living,

172
00:12:23,621 --> 00:12:27,620
and I would always say, you know, I make CDs and records,

173
00:12:27,621 --> 00:12:31,620
and I would always hear, they still make records?

174
00:12:31,621 --> 00:12:34,620
And I'd tell them how many we make, and they were always amazed.

175
00:12:34,621 --> 00:12:37,620
Recently, I was at a party sitting at a table,

176
00:12:37,621 --> 00:12:41,920
And same conversation, I said, we make records, CDs.

177
00:12:42,260 --> 00:12:44,460
And the comment back to me was, they still make CDs?

178
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,780
Long before the days of television and recorded music,

179
00:12:49,480 --> 00:12:52,520
the masses turned to the printed word for entertainment and

180
00:12:52,521 --> 00:12:53,221
enlightenment.

181
00:12:55,380 --> 00:12:59,260
The comforting clack of the keyboard, the familiar smell and

182
00:12:59,261 --> 00:13:00,120
feel of the paper.

183
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:02,560
Would these be on the way out, too?

184
00:13:02,561 --> 00:13:13,980
In 2008, Americans discarded nearly 700,000 tons worth of tube TVs, of which only 18% were recycled.

185
00:13:15,120 --> 00:13:16,266
The bottom line?

186
00:13:16,267 --> 00:13:21,280
More than 46,000 tons of lead found its way back into the environment.

187
00:13:23,360 --> 00:13:26,100
Retro Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

188
00:13:26,101 --> 00:13:34,220
As we said goodbye to our turntables and rabbit ears, we welcomed a new era, the computer

189
00:13:34,221 --> 00:13:36,720
era.

190
00:13:36,721 --> 00:13:42,480
The modern computer is always evolving, but one thing has remained constant, the trusty

191
00:13:42,481 --> 00:13:44,800
keyboard.

192
00:13:44,801 --> 00:13:51,720
The birthplace of the keyboard, the typewriter, became obsolete in the 1980s, but only after

193
00:13:51,721 --> 00:13:54,820
as one of the most ubiquitous machines of the 20th century.

194
00:13:57,260 --> 00:13:59,720
The typewriter definitely was a revolution.

195
00:14:00,020 --> 00:14:01,900
Before that, you had to handwrite everything,

196
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:06,260
and a good typist can type faster than the fastest writer can write.

197
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:10,080
Let's face it, something that I write may not be able to even be read by anyone,

198
00:14:10,360 --> 00:14:13,100
but from a typewriter, you can all read what's typed.

199
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:17,160
The typewriter attracted women into the workforce

200
00:14:17,161 --> 00:14:20,220
and brought structure and uniformity to the business world.

201
00:14:21,720 --> 00:14:27,000
It became a virtual appendage of writers, secretaries, and news reporters.

202
00:14:30,360 --> 00:14:33,560
American inventor Christopher Scholz and two partners

203
00:14:33,561 --> 00:14:37,720
developed the first commercially manufactured typewriter in 1874.

204
00:14:39,300 --> 00:14:42,060
They had Remington and Sons,

205
00:14:42,260 --> 00:14:45,920
who was a manufacturer of guns and sewing machines,

206
00:14:46,140 --> 00:14:47,880
make their first typewriters.

207
00:14:47,881 --> 00:14:54,160
The odd-looking contraption was more of a curiosity at first, and slow to be accepted,

208
00:14:54,680 --> 00:14:58,420
because business and personal correspondence had always been conducted in longhand.

209
00:14:59,960 --> 00:15:05,020
Mark Twain, who was reportedly the first to submit a typewritten manuscript, despised

210
00:15:05,021 --> 00:15:06,560
it because he considered it awkward.

211
00:15:08,560 --> 00:15:12,540
Even the earliest Remingtons boasted that initially confusing letter-key layout.

212
00:15:12,541 --> 00:15:20,940
The QWERTY keyboard, so called for the first six letters on the top row, became the universally accepted design.

213
00:15:22,560 --> 00:15:29,060
Some say this arrangement allowed salesmen to type the word typewriter more easily when demonstrating the product,

214
00:15:29,480 --> 00:15:31,620
because all the required letters are in the top row.

215
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:41,080
But the more practical explanation is that frequently used letters had to be separated, so the keys wouldn't stick.

216
00:15:42,540 --> 00:15:48,480
Early on, the machine went through some design modifications.

217
00:15:48,481 --> 00:15:53,180
We press the key and we can see exactly where it's hitting the platen.

218
00:15:53,181 --> 00:15:55,760
So this was called a visible typewriter.

219
00:15:55,761 --> 00:15:58,380
This particular one is a Remington 10.

220
00:15:58,381 --> 00:16:06,580
Was basically the design that predominated for the next 60 years or so.

221
00:16:06,581 --> 00:16:12,400
In the 1930s, companies began developing electric typewriters with limited success and only

222
00:16:12,401 --> 00:16:14,360
in the business market.

223
00:16:14,361 --> 00:16:18,880
The huge development in typewriters was in 1961

224
00:16:18,881 --> 00:16:21,540
when the IBM Selectrix came out.

225
00:16:21,541 --> 00:16:25,080
It had a single golf ball type shaped element

226
00:16:25,081 --> 00:16:28,880
and you press the key and it would spin to the correct letter

227
00:16:28,881 --> 00:16:30,080
and then strike.

228
00:16:30,081 --> 00:16:35,260
Then in the early 1970s, IBM created the correcting

229
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:37,100
Selectrix typewriter.

230
00:16:37,101 --> 00:16:41,760
Because before that time, you either had to use a racer,

231
00:16:41,761 --> 00:16:45,240
which usually didn't work too well and got a lot of eraser

232
00:16:45,241 --> 00:16:47,940
crumbs in the typewriter.

233
00:16:47,941 --> 00:16:51,700
Or you had to use something like liquid correcting fluid.

234
00:16:55,700 --> 00:16:57,300
One version of the correcting typewriter

235
00:16:57,301 --> 00:17:01,180
contained a cartridge with an ink-coated cloth ribbon.

236
00:17:01,181 --> 00:17:04,660
Another was a correctable carbon film.

237
00:17:04,661 --> 00:17:07,720
After a typo, the user hit the corrector key,

238
00:17:07,721 --> 00:17:11,320
followed by the mistyped letter key.

239
00:17:11,321 --> 00:17:15,700
To correct ink-coated ribbon, a cover-up tape strikes the mistake making it disappear.

240
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:20,220
A carbon film error could be lifted right off the paper.

241
00:17:22,800 --> 00:17:27,580
This primitive delete function may have extended the typewriter's lifespan by a few years.

242
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:33,460
But once computers took hold in the mid-1980s, there was no fighting progress.

243
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:40,120
The mighty typewriter that served us so well for nearly a century had fallen by the wayside.

244
00:17:41,320 --> 00:17:45,320
A similar fate befell another printing icon developed in the

245
00:17:45,321 --> 00:17:51,320
late 1800s, the mimeograph. The mimeograph employed an

246
00:17:51,321 --> 00:17:56,320
ink-fed drum, around which a cut waxed stencil rotated as

247
00:17:56,321 --> 00:18:00,320
successive sheets of paper were fed into it.

248
00:18:00,321 --> 00:18:03,320
Some of us might remember having to make copies in the old

249
00:18:03,321 --> 00:18:07,320
days by turning a crank, getting blue on your fingers, and just

250
00:18:07,321 --> 00:18:10,320
trying to get those copies made, smelling that smell of not just

251
00:18:10,321 --> 00:18:12,820
that smell of not just the ink but the paper itself.

252
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:15,020
And of course, they didn't look all that good.

253
00:18:16,460 --> 00:18:22,940
Then in 1959, a revolutionary invention from the company whose name became synonymous with copying

254
00:18:22,941 --> 00:18:25,040
spelled doom for the mimeograph.

255
00:18:27,480 --> 00:18:35,120
The Xerox 914 copier combined photoconductivity with electrostatic printing, changing business forever.

256
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:39,640
We went from a mimeograph where you had to turn a drum to being able to push a button

257
00:18:39,641 --> 00:18:41,780
and make a thousand copies on a Xerox machine

258
00:18:41,781 --> 00:18:45,080
and have them stapled and collated

259
00:18:45,081 --> 00:18:48,360
to now making digital copies of things

260
00:18:48,361 --> 00:18:51,060
that maybe will never even be a paper copy.

261
00:18:52,340 --> 00:18:56,040
But there's one industry that must continue relying on paper copies,

262
00:18:56,760 --> 00:18:57,920
at least for the time being.

263
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:04,540
Since the first continuously published newspaper emerged in 1704,

264
00:19:05,880 --> 00:19:07,820
Americans have looked to their trusty gazettes

265
00:19:07,821 --> 00:19:12,720
to keep them aware of the goings-on in their community, their country, and the world.

266
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:19,820
Reading the morning paper over coffee is a deep-rooted ritual that prepares us for the day to come.

267
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:28,120
But after three centuries of success, does the newspaper have an uncertain future?

268
00:19:28,121 --> 00:19:38,200
The top 25 American daily papers posted an average circulation drop of 7% between 2007 and 2008.

269
00:19:39,080 --> 00:19:39,780
But why?

270
00:19:40,680 --> 00:19:42,420
In a word, competition.

271
00:19:43,400 --> 00:19:48,100
Newspapers have encountered competition before, but now they're creating it.

272
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:51,060
The Houston Chronicle.

273
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:55,020
One of the top 10 U.S. dailies is owned by the Hearst Corporation,

274
00:19:55,021 --> 00:19:57,300
one of this network's parent companies.

275
00:19:58,120 --> 00:20:00,760
The paper launched its website in 1994.

276
00:20:01,780 --> 00:20:04,160
There's been radio, there's been television,

277
00:20:04,161 --> 00:20:06,200
and today we see the Internet.

278
00:20:06,740 --> 00:20:08,900
But what we've decided to do this time

279
00:20:08,901 --> 00:20:11,620
is to embrace and coexist with the Internet

280
00:20:11,621 --> 00:20:14,620
and use technology to our advantage.

281
00:20:15,720 --> 00:20:18,100
Print and online have joined forces.

282
00:20:18,720 --> 00:20:21,080
The reaction of the people in the newsroom,

283
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:24,440
they saw the Web as an interesting hobby

284
00:20:24,441 --> 00:20:25,500
that we had as a newspaper.

285
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:28,100
What's really changed is it's become mainstream.

286
00:20:28,120 --> 00:20:31,120
These days, the Houston Chronicle's website,

287
00:20:31,121 --> 00:20:36,120
Kron.com, averages about 80 million page views per month,

288
00:20:36,121 --> 00:20:39,120
while circulation of the print edition hovers around half a

289
00:20:39,121 --> 00:20:44,120
million per weekday. Under the supervision of the

290
00:20:44,121 --> 00:20:48,120
Chronicle's editor-in-chief, both news entities collaborate

291
00:20:48,121 --> 00:20:51,120
in an endeavor reliant on hundreds of reporters.

292
00:20:51,121 --> 00:20:55,120
For them, the lines between print and online are blurred,

293
00:20:55,121 --> 00:20:58,120
are blurred, since both editions depend on their work.

294
00:20:58,121 --> 00:21:00,900
We're leading now with the big increase in the leak

295
00:21:00,901 --> 00:21:01,760
in the Dow.

296
00:21:01,761 --> 00:21:05,840
19 people are probably buried and dead in the rubble.

297
00:21:05,841 --> 00:21:09,200
We still have the wind, the energy story.

298
00:21:09,201 --> 00:21:11,060
They go out and they cover events.

299
00:21:11,061 --> 00:21:14,240
And then in a very tight window of time,

300
00:21:14,241 --> 00:21:16,040
they're bringing that story back.

301
00:21:16,041 --> 00:21:19,380
A headline writer is putting a compelling headline on it.

302
00:21:19,381 --> 00:21:21,880
Illustrators and page layout people

303
00:21:21,881 --> 00:21:24,820
are making the stories attractive and visually

304
00:21:24,821 --> 00:21:28,300
pleasing. They're adding a contemporary look.

305
00:21:28,301 --> 00:21:31,780
The finalized layout goes to the print plant,

306
00:21:32,100 --> 00:21:36,723
where these giant presses spit out papers at a dizzying speed.

307
00:21:36,724 --> 00:21:38,960
And then it is on the doorstep

308
00:21:38,961 --> 00:21:41,987
of the reader by 5.30 in the morning.

309
00:21:41,988 --> 00:21:46,240
Now, compare the expenses of the printed daily paper

310
00:21:46,241 --> 00:21:48,188
to the online version.

311
00:21:48,189 --> 00:21:54,120
We use about 200 metric tons of newsprint every day and about 9,000

312
00:21:54,121 --> 00:21:57,800
pounds of ink a day.

313
00:21:57,801 --> 00:22:01,120
A newspaper website has servers that maybe cost thousands of dollars.

314
00:22:01,121 --> 00:22:03,960
It doesn't have multi-million dollar presses.

315
00:22:03,961 --> 00:22:06,740
It does not have the delivery costs that a newspaper does.

316
00:22:06,741 --> 00:22:11,000
You don't have the trucks and the rising gas prices to worry about.

317
00:22:11,001 --> 00:22:15,420
And online offers another big advantage that print does not.

318
00:22:15,421 --> 00:22:19,960
The biggest difference between online and print is that print is a snapshot of a point

319
00:22:19,961 --> 00:22:20,960
in time.

320
00:22:20,961 --> 00:22:24,120
It's a picture of what the world looked like at 10.30 last night.

321
00:22:24,121 --> 00:22:26,640
The website is continually being updated throughout the day.

322
00:22:27,260 --> 00:22:29,700
To feed the online edition as well as print,

323
00:22:30,360 --> 00:22:31,840
journalists have had to adapt.

324
00:22:32,920 --> 00:22:35,300
When I was a reporter many years ago,

325
00:22:35,680 --> 00:22:40,220
I could spend most of the day writing a 750-word story.

326
00:22:40,760 --> 00:22:42,300
But today, that's not the case.

327
00:22:43,080 --> 00:22:44,260
With the development of the web,

328
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:46,440
virtually every job in the newsroom has changed.

329
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:48,200
Reporters have become bloggers.

330
00:22:48,420 --> 00:22:49,980
Photographers have become videographers.

331
00:22:50,700 --> 00:22:52,400
Editors have become web producers.

332
00:22:52,401 --> 00:22:57,400
Even our editorial cartoonist today is producing animations in 3-D.

333
00:22:57,401 --> 00:23:01,400
So are newspapers ready to pull the plug on the press?

334
00:23:01,401 --> 00:23:05,400
Today, if we shut down the printed newspaper and just published a website,

335
00:23:05,401 --> 00:23:09,400
we'd be isolating ourselves from about a third of the population in Houston.

336
00:23:09,401 --> 00:23:12,400
Because as much as the Internet has become ubiquitous,

337
00:23:12,401 --> 00:23:15,400
it has not reached everyone.

338
00:23:15,401 --> 00:23:19,400
But how long will the two editions continue to coexist?

339
00:23:19,401 --> 00:23:27,700
There are a lot of people, 78 million baby boomers to be precise, that are using both

340
00:23:27,701 --> 00:23:32,960
right now. But the young teenagers of today, they are growing up fully digital.

341
00:23:32,961 --> 00:23:39,900
We're in the middle of a media revolution, and revolutions don't end in a tie.

342
00:23:39,901 --> 00:23:44,360
Still, there will always be something soothing about doing the Sunday crossword

343
00:23:44,361 --> 00:23:47,416
puzzle in pen. Okay, maybe pencil.

344
00:23:47,417 --> 00:23:52,720
Whichever your preference, you have to admit that web-based

345
00:23:52,721 --> 00:23:58,680
and digital technologies are taking over our lives, thanks in no small part to some handy

346
00:23:58,681 --> 00:24:03,199
gadgets we suddenly can't live without.

347
00:24:03,200 --> 00:24:08,760
In 1951, Jack Kerouac typed, On the Road, during

348
00:24:08,761 --> 00:24:10,273
a three-week marathon.

349
00:24:10,274 --> 00:24:15,500
To minimize interruption, he taped pages of paper together in a continuously

350
00:24:15,501 --> 00:24:24,264
feeding role. The final single-spaced manuscript was a scroll nearly 120 feet long.

351
00:24:24,265 --> 00:24:25,320
Retro Tech

352
00:24:25,321 --> 00:24:32,867
will return on Modern Marvels. The MP3 player replaced the Discman.

353
00:24:32,868 --> 00:24:35,120
The Discman replaced

354
00:24:35,121 --> 00:24:40,620
the Walkman, and the Walkman replaced the turntable.

355
00:24:40,621 --> 00:24:43,260
No matter how much we loved each one,

356
00:24:43,261 --> 00:24:45,580
the availability of more compact options

357
00:24:45,581 --> 00:24:48,380
was certainly attractive.

358
00:24:48,381 --> 00:24:53,220
The same held true for our cameras, phones, and organizers.

359
00:24:53,221 --> 00:24:56,360
The first cameras were definitely not personal cameras.

360
00:24:56,361 --> 00:25:00,360
It was a big, giant device that went on a big, giant tripod.

361
00:25:00,361 --> 00:25:01,980
You had to be a pro.

362
00:25:01,981 --> 00:25:04,420
But then as they were able to make the cameras smaller

363
00:25:04,421 --> 00:25:07,760
and smaller, it started to become something that you and I could do.

364
00:25:09,020 --> 00:25:11,840
Capturing and sharing memories became family traditions.

365
00:25:12,620 --> 00:25:18,360
But it wasn't always so convenient until one company indulged our need for instant gratification.

366
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:26,100
You bought a roll of film, put it in a camera, and when the film was all exposed, you took

367
00:25:26,101 --> 00:25:27,540
it to the drugstore, you mailed it away.

368
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:30,040
The Model 95 was introduced in 1948.

369
00:25:30,041 --> 00:25:38,180
And with the introduction of this camera and Polaroid instant film, we could produce pictures right on the spot, wherever you were.

370
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:42,860
Instant pictures. Today we take them for granted.

371
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:46,760
But in 1948, the Polaroid was revolutionary.

372
00:25:49,500 --> 00:25:52,700
The user took the picture, then pulled the tab.

373
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:56,560
The negative and receiving sheet slid through a set of rollers,

374
00:25:56,561 --> 00:26:00,580
causing a pod of chemicals embedded in the receiving sheet to burst.

375
00:26:01,920 --> 00:26:04,800
The rollers distributed the chemicals across the negative,

376
00:26:05,720 --> 00:26:08,180
triggering a chemical reaction that processed the image.

377
00:26:10,060 --> 00:26:13,300
90 seconds later, the negative was peeled away and discarded.

378
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:19,380
In 1972, Polaroid introduced the SX-70,

379
00:26:19,820 --> 00:26:24,220
which was able to develop images without tabs to pull and negatives to peel.

380
00:26:24,221 --> 00:26:29,220
It wasn't that long ago that we had a separate camera, phone,

381
00:26:29,221 --> 00:26:34,220
address book, calendar, music player, and computer.

382
00:26:34,221 --> 00:26:38,220
But today the market is flooded with feature-laden combination

383
00:26:38,221 --> 00:26:42,220
gadgets that fit in the palm of your hand, each rendering a

384
00:26:42,221 --> 00:26:48,220
previous version obsolete. The aptly named Palm, Inc.,

385
00:26:48,221 --> 00:26:51,220
in Sunnyvale, California, pioneered the granddaddy of

386
00:26:51,221 --> 00:26:54,020
the granddaddy of must-have pocket tools.

387
00:26:54,021 --> 00:26:56,920
In 1997, we came out with the Palm Pilot.

388
00:26:56,921 --> 00:27:01,860
This really represented cultural phenomena in mobile computing

389
00:27:01,861 --> 00:27:06,060
because it gave you a way of entering information on the go

390
00:27:06,061 --> 00:27:08,080
without a full keyboard.

391
00:27:08,081 --> 00:27:10,080
It was a stylus-based interaction

392
00:27:10,081 --> 00:27:12,400
where you would actually write in characters.

393
00:27:12,401 --> 00:27:15,080
It actually forced you to learn a very specific way

394
00:27:15,081 --> 00:27:16,680
to write your letters.

395
00:27:18,580 --> 00:27:20,180
Despite the added effort required

396
00:27:20,181 --> 00:27:25,700
on the part of the user, this glorified address book was surprisingly successful and entered

397
00:27:25,701 --> 00:27:31,780
the vernacular as a synonym for personal digital assistants, or PDAs, regardless of the brand.

398
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:37,120
The original Palm Pilot had your contacts, it had tasks and memos, basic calculator,

399
00:27:37,121 --> 00:27:41,787
and it organized it into one place with one magical killer app.

400
00:27:41,788 --> 00:27:44,380
That killer app was synchronization

401
00:27:44,381 --> 00:27:48,200
with your computer, so all of that information was protected.

402
00:27:48,201 --> 00:27:55,200
While the Palm Pilot was organizing your life, the cell phone was keeping you in touch.

403
00:27:55,201 --> 00:28:01,200
By today's standards, the early versions were pricey, unreliable, and goofy looking.

404
00:28:01,201 --> 00:28:05,200
But it was freedom from the cord, and that was worth it.

405
00:28:05,201 --> 00:28:10,200
If you had one of these, you were pretty cool.

406
00:28:10,201 --> 00:28:15,200
The evolution of cellular phones rendered some old faithfuls obsolete.

407
00:28:15,201 --> 00:28:19,900
from rotary dial models and pay phones to car phones.

408
00:28:19,901 --> 00:28:23,160
Some even predict the demise of the hallowed landline,

409
00:28:23,161 --> 00:28:26,460
the cornerstone of modern communication that served us so

410
00:28:26,461 --> 00:28:28,700
well since the days of Alexander Graham Bell.

411
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:36,060
Today, the cell phone meets the organizer, meets the computer,

412
00:28:36,061 --> 00:28:37,960
and a bunch of other features.

413
00:28:37,961 --> 00:28:39,780
It's called the smartphone.

414
00:28:39,781 --> 00:28:42,980
And unlike its ancestors, it has a keyboard.

415
00:28:42,981 --> 00:28:45,480
And it's still smaller.

416
00:28:45,481 --> 00:28:50,520
Keyboards are a big story because they are the transition

417
00:28:50,521 --> 00:28:52,120
from handhelds to smartphones.

418
00:28:52,121 --> 00:28:57,600
The unconventional wisdom here is that really big, full-size

419
00:28:57,601 --> 00:29:01,000
keyboards work, but tiny keyboards work just as well

420
00:29:01,001 --> 00:29:03,600
because you're using your thumbs.

421
00:29:03,601 --> 00:29:06,440
The smartphone is a nail in the coffin for many of our trusty

422
00:29:06,441 --> 00:29:11,100
devices, because it really is the sum of its parts.

423
00:29:11,101 --> 00:29:15,500
Our first real iconic phone, which was the Trio 600, in 2003.

424
00:29:15,501 --> 00:29:18,500
It was one of the first true smartphones.

425
00:29:18,501 --> 00:29:26,080
And then in 2007, we delivered Centro, which is putting an incredible amount of power into

426
00:29:26,081 --> 00:29:29,900
a tiny package but also making it really inexpensive and accessible.

427
00:29:29,901 --> 00:29:33,780
The phone is evolving in not one direction but in multiple directions.

428
00:29:33,781 --> 00:29:35,720
It's going to be your bank.

429
00:29:35,721 --> 00:29:37,380
It's going to be your credit card.

430
00:29:37,381 --> 00:29:39,340
It's going to be your educational tool.

431
00:29:39,341 --> 00:29:41,100
It's going to be your training tool.

432
00:29:41,101 --> 00:29:43,760
It's going to be your radio. It's going to be your keys.

433
00:29:44,200 --> 00:29:47,140
It's going to be how you buy things, how you board a plane.

434
00:29:47,260 --> 00:29:49,360
This is going to be your ticket for your next airplane flight.

435
00:29:49,740 --> 00:29:54,500
You see, it's evolving into many, many aspects of different infrastructure

436
00:29:54,501 --> 00:29:56,720
that will be all merged together in a wireless way

437
00:29:56,721 --> 00:29:59,760
because that phone is going to get smarter and smarter.

438
00:30:00,940 --> 00:30:05,100
But will all these advantages render face-to-face human interaction obsolete?

439
00:30:06,180 --> 00:30:09,920
The biggest problem with smartphones is we don't always have smart users.

440
00:30:09,921 --> 00:30:14,960
We get addicted to the phone so much that we forget a very important thing.

441
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:18,120
There's a time to plug in and a time to unplug.

442
00:30:19,460 --> 00:30:25,340
The computer chips used to get your information around are the same ones that control how you get around.

443
00:30:26,280 --> 00:30:30,440
But while some things go obsolete, they'll never go out of style.

444
00:30:32,720 --> 00:30:38,320
Thomas Edison lobbied successfully for the word hello to be used as the standard telephone greeting.

445
00:30:38,321 --> 00:30:41,460
much to the dismay of Alexander Graham Bell,

446
00:30:42,080 --> 00:30:44,840
who preferred Ahoy! Ahoy!

447
00:30:45,940 --> 00:30:48,740
Retro Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

448
00:30:52,160 --> 00:30:55,580
Everyone remembers their first love and their first car.

449
00:30:56,760 --> 00:30:58,780
For some, they were one and the same.

450
00:31:00,800 --> 00:31:03,720
Few car aficionados are more enamored with their vehicles

451
00:31:03,721 --> 00:31:05,280
than classic Mustang lovers.

452
00:31:05,281 --> 00:31:11,520
They drive their collectibles, shine them up, show them off, and discuss them with other

453
00:31:11,521 --> 00:31:14,700
classic Mustang lovers.

454
00:31:14,701 --> 00:31:19,440
But underneath the hoods of their retro babies is an outdated technology.

455
00:31:19,441 --> 00:31:26,240
What we have here today is two Shelby's, 1968 KR 500, 2009 GT 500.

456
00:31:26,241 --> 00:31:30,600
We are going to show you the difference of technology from yesterday to today.

457
00:31:30,601 --> 00:31:33,220
Starting with this 68 KR right here, let's open the hood here.

458
00:31:33,221 --> 00:31:39,020
It's got a 428 Cobra Jet, normally carbureted motor in it.

459
00:31:40,160 --> 00:31:45,220
Up until the 1980s, most cars relied on a carburetor to power the combustion engine.

460
00:31:47,200 --> 00:31:51,520
Air travels in from outside, past a filter, and into the carburetor.

461
00:31:53,480 --> 00:31:59,740
Pressure on the gas pedal allows a regulated amount of air and a regulated amount of fuel into the chamber where they mix.

462
00:31:59,741 --> 00:32:05,300
The throttle valve allows the air-fuel blend to enter the engine.

463
00:32:06,660 --> 00:32:08,900
Over here, fuel injection.

464
00:32:09,180 --> 00:32:11,120
You have fuel injectors on each cylinder.

465
00:32:12,020 --> 00:32:13,160
You have a fuel rail.

466
00:32:13,300 --> 00:32:14,560
You have an equalizer tube.

467
00:32:14,840 --> 00:32:16,080
Like there, you have a throttle body.

468
00:32:16,180 --> 00:32:20,440
You have an electronic fuel equalizer pressurized pump right here.

469
00:32:21,160 --> 00:32:24,060
So what's the upside to this complicated fuel injection system?

470
00:32:24,620 --> 00:32:26,460
This car can get 25 miles a gallon.

471
00:32:26,740 --> 00:32:28,560
This could probably get 12 to 15.

472
00:32:28,561 --> 00:32:31,220
And the reason is, is because all the extra excess fuel

473
00:32:31,221 --> 00:32:32,760
that's not burned gets thrown out the back,

474
00:32:32,761 --> 00:32:34,260
and it smokes, and it smells good,

475
00:32:34,261 --> 00:32:36,100
and everybody goes, oh, my God, I want an old car.

476
00:32:36,101 --> 00:32:37,560
Here, you don't smell anything,

477
00:32:37,561 --> 00:32:39,400
because it's engineering perfect.

478
00:32:39,401 --> 00:32:43,400
So it actually burns every last minuscule of fuel and air.

479
00:32:43,401 --> 00:32:44,600
That's the good part.

480
00:32:44,601 --> 00:32:47,120
The bad part is, I can work on this,

481
00:32:47,121 --> 00:32:48,620
and a lot of people can work on this.

482
00:32:48,621 --> 00:32:53,620
And here, I need, like, an engineering degree and a laptop.

483
00:32:53,621 --> 00:32:57,820
Just another case of mechanical versus computerized.

484
00:32:57,821 --> 00:33:04,180
For some, no computer chips can ever replace the soulful roar of the engine and the familiar smell of exhaust.

485
00:33:06,860 --> 00:33:09,960
It's not just the insides of cars that demanded a makeover.

486
00:33:11,900 --> 00:33:13,660
Bumpers and paint have also evolved.

487
00:33:15,240 --> 00:33:19,260
Imagine today's freeways full of these lead-painted steel bumper dinosaurs.

488
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:25,300
Some of the interesting ingredients that have been used in the paint are metal flakes,

489
00:33:25,301 --> 00:33:29,040
fish scales, which were added to give another kind of iridescent look.

490
00:33:29,500 --> 00:33:32,320
Base paint used to be lead, then they went to a water base.

491
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:38,780
Body styles and bumper designs have changed to reflect tastes and ever-improving safety regulations.

492
00:33:39,640 --> 00:33:44,460
Back in the 1970s, when we started the heavy focus on safety standards,

493
00:33:44,640 --> 00:33:47,260
we went to the 5-mile-per-hour bumpers,

494
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:53,520
which, when applied to existing automobile designs of the time, were pretty unattractive.

495
00:33:53,521 --> 00:34:02,872
In the 70s, we had shock absorber bumpers, and at the same time, we started with the rubber across the front chrome of the bumpers, which became a standard later on in

496
00:34:02,873 --> 00:34:04,160
the design of the cars.

497
00:34:04,460 --> 00:34:09,320
This is an 89 Porsche Speedster with the heavy rubber across here, and no chrome to the car.

498
00:34:10,120 --> 00:34:19,260
This is an 06 Ford Mustang, and here you can see the fiberglass bumper as opposed to the chrome bumpers, and no rubber integrated here.

499
00:34:19,400 --> 00:34:21,160
It's all part of the whole design of the car.

500
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:30,640
But first things first, before you can enjoy your car's fuel injections and safety features,

501
00:34:30,641 --> 00:34:33,200
you have to get in.

502
00:34:33,201 --> 00:34:34,520
Even that has changed.

503
00:34:34,521 --> 00:34:38,480
Nearly gone are the days of the metal door and ignition key.

504
00:34:38,481 --> 00:34:45,740
You still have a driver's side key entry, but you don't see a door lock anywhere on

505
00:34:45,741 --> 00:34:47,520
the passenger side door.

506
00:34:47,521 --> 00:34:52,200
In due course, we're going to see an end to the ignition key, door key.

507
00:34:53,720 --> 00:34:58,540
The keyless remote, first available in the early 1980s, got us halfway there.

508
00:35:00,720 --> 00:35:05,600
The latest high-end cars to roll off the assembly line incorporate something far more intuitive.

509
00:35:07,420 --> 00:35:11,880
This car was locked, but it sensed my presence and it now is unlocked.

510
00:35:12,300 --> 00:35:14,300
How it did that is with this smart key.

511
00:35:14,920 --> 00:35:16,400
I didn't have to take that out of my pocket.

512
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:19,020
Don't have to do anything but just keep it on my body.

513
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:25,740
It'll actually open the car for me, and to start it, I just sit in the car, hit the button, it starts itself.

514
00:35:25,900 --> 00:35:28,400
Don't have to stick this in anything. It's called smart key technology.

515
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:32,680
Perhaps someday all doors will unlock instinctively.

516
00:35:33,460 --> 00:35:37,200
But until then, this plastic key card isn't a bad option.

517
00:35:37,201 --> 00:35:45,820
It's what has made millions of metal hotel room keys and those obligatory dangling numbered key chains obsolete.

518
00:35:48,160 --> 00:35:52,780
In the late 1970s, to combat key theft and subsequent hotel crime,

519
00:35:53,540 --> 00:36:00,360
the lodging industry started to do away with the standard metal key in favor of a reprogrammable disposable solution.

520
00:36:00,361 --> 00:36:07,720
The Flamingo Hotel in Las Vegas has over 3,400 rooms

521
00:36:07,721 --> 00:36:11,220
and issues 2.4 million key cards each year.

522
00:36:12,560 --> 00:36:15,620
Upon registration, we gain access to the customer's reservation.

523
00:36:17,760 --> 00:36:19,700
Actually go to the key-making application,

524
00:36:20,180 --> 00:36:21,360
punch in for a brand-new key,

525
00:36:21,920 --> 00:36:24,240
and we're all set.

526
00:36:24,241 --> 00:36:34,480
The computer encodes the card's magnetic strip with the check-in, check-out dates, as well

527
00:36:34,481 --> 00:36:41,120
as the particular room's sequential numeric access code.

528
00:36:41,121 --> 00:36:47,560
The locks understand what key is due to come in based on the previous customer's sequence.

529
00:36:47,561 --> 00:36:51,560
Since we cut this key downstairs just now, this would be the next one in the sequence.

530
00:36:51,561 --> 00:36:53,640
you'd get the green light to be able to access the guest room.

531
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:59,080
If you were to try to access the room through a non-sequential or an expired key,

532
00:36:59,480 --> 00:37:03,380
you'd be getting the yellow light, which would not allow you access.

533
00:37:03,820 --> 00:37:07,140
If you improperly insert the key into the guest lock, you would get a red light.

534
00:37:09,080 --> 00:37:13,560
400,000 of the key cards the Flamingo issues each year are never returned.

535
00:37:15,100 --> 00:37:18,760
And at four cents a pop, that costs the hotel 16 grand.

536
00:37:18,761 --> 00:37:25,760
But it's a small price to pay for the increased security and convenience over metal keys.

537
00:37:25,761 --> 00:37:31,760
So how long before the old faithful metal key goes completely extinct?

538
00:37:31,761 --> 00:37:37,760
Physical metal keys that we deal with today will increasingly become obsolete, but it will be slow.

539
00:37:37,761 --> 00:37:43,760
The reason it will be slow is there are a lot of us that still have homes and still have devices that need metal keys.

540
00:37:43,761 --> 00:37:47,300
and let's face it, to change all of the locks in your home,

541
00:37:47,700 --> 00:37:49,880
a lot of people are not going to do that very quickly.

542
00:37:51,520 --> 00:37:55,460
And there's something to be said about the secure feeling of hearing that lock turn.

543
00:37:58,200 --> 00:38:02,000
Once your equipment needs replacing and you finally spring for the new stuff,

544
00:38:02,480 --> 00:38:07,820
how do you make sure no one gets access to the personal info on your old stuff?

545
00:38:07,821 --> 00:38:14,700
In 2007, a 1966 Shelby Cobra 427 Super Snake

546
00:38:14,701 --> 00:38:18,120
fetched a world record auction price for an American car,

547
00:38:18,720 --> 00:38:20,160
$5.5 million.

548
00:38:21,040 --> 00:38:23,540
Retro Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

549
00:38:25,180 --> 00:38:29,160
If your favorite music and video still live on the obsolete formats

550
00:38:29,161 --> 00:38:31,820
and your players bite the proverbial dust,

551
00:38:32,540 --> 00:38:33,280
are you out of luck?

552
00:38:34,440 --> 00:38:35,440
Fear not.

553
00:38:35,441 --> 00:38:39,520
There is a way to salvage and preserve your treasures in more current formats

554
00:38:39,521 --> 00:38:42,800
by using media transfer hardware and software.

555
00:38:45,340 --> 00:38:50,500
We recognized that everyone had some of the old media,

556
00:38:50,740 --> 00:38:53,460
either LPs, VHS tapes, tape cassettes,

557
00:38:53,660 --> 00:38:56,360
and they were storing them, but they weren't playing them.

558
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:02,320
The actual hardware to play back this medium is disappearing.

559
00:39:02,560 --> 00:39:05,420
That's when we conceived of the idea of transfer technology.

560
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:08,100
turning this into something you could use.

561
00:39:08,240 --> 00:39:10,240
Basically, it takes an analog format

562
00:39:10,241 --> 00:39:13,420
and transfers it into a digital format.

563
00:39:14,080 --> 00:39:17,220
So it's kind of like old school meets new school.

564
00:39:17,740 --> 00:39:20,260
The LP-DUG is the easiest way to convert music

565
00:39:20,261 --> 00:39:21,320
from vinyl to digital.

566
00:39:21,620 --> 00:39:23,480
It's actually going to record a WAV file

567
00:39:23,481 --> 00:39:27,260
that you can then process in your computer if you wanted to

568
00:39:27,261 --> 00:39:29,960
or just for easy play on your iPod.

569
00:39:31,620 --> 00:39:33,100
Anticipating that some customers

570
00:39:33,101 --> 00:39:34,800
might be technologically challenged,

571
00:39:34,801 --> 00:39:40,400
They've made it as simple as installing the software on your computer and following a few easy steps.

572
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:49,020
Once my recording is done, I can just go ahead and type the name of the artist, name of the album, and the track title.

573
00:39:50,660 --> 00:39:54,020
The tape-to-PC cassette converter works much the same way.

574
00:39:57,780 --> 00:40:03,220
And now, once in here, I can just throw away this tape because it's all digital.

575
00:40:04,800 --> 00:40:07,300
But not all memories are audio.

576
00:40:07,301 --> 00:40:10,300
Odds are you still have a few VHS tapes of cherished home

577
00:40:10,301 --> 00:40:14,000
movies lying around your house.

578
00:40:14,001 --> 00:40:20,640
With the VCR to PC, all of this can now fit onto this.

579
00:40:20,641 --> 00:40:23,160
Where you can take thousands of videos and just put them on

580
00:40:23,161 --> 00:40:24,060
your hard drive.

581
00:40:24,061 --> 00:40:26,760
And once I have it on my hard drive, I can go ahead and burn

582
00:40:26,761 --> 00:40:31,960
it into a DVD or put it into a portable device.

583
00:40:31,961 --> 00:40:34,880
All your old stuff can now live on your new computer.

584
00:40:35,820 --> 00:40:39,580
But what happens in the future when that new computer is suddenly old?

585
00:40:41,720 --> 00:40:46,260
It's inevitable that you'll eventually bid farewell to your once-treasured electronic gadgets.

586
00:40:47,740 --> 00:40:48,880
You have a choice.

587
00:40:49,180 --> 00:40:51,700
You can toss them into the drawer or the trash,

588
00:40:51,880 --> 00:40:55,700
or you can give them the respectful and responsible end they deserve

589
00:40:55,701 --> 00:41:00,140
by taking your old equipment to an electronics recycling facility,

590
00:41:00,420 --> 00:41:01,740
like E-Waste Center.

591
00:41:01,960 --> 00:41:04,760
in Commerce, California.

592
00:41:04,761 --> 00:41:07,700
Our facility here is about 40,000 square feet,

593
00:41:07,701 --> 00:41:10,900
and we take in anywhere from 200,000 to 300,000 pounds

594
00:41:10,901 --> 00:41:13,700
of e-waste a month.

595
00:41:13,701 --> 00:41:16,040
This gadget graveyard serves as a collection hub

596
00:41:16,041 --> 00:41:21,780
for discarded tech, including TVs, computers, office equipment,

597
00:41:21,781 --> 00:41:24,820
and cell phones.

598
00:41:24,821 --> 00:41:28,180
The most common item we get here are TV CRTs,

599
00:41:28,181 --> 00:41:31,720
and we get about 5,000 of them a month.

600
00:41:31,721 --> 00:41:34,860
These TVs are among the 27 million that were taken out of

601
00:41:34,861 --> 00:41:37,200
service in the U.S. in 2007.

602
00:41:39,660 --> 00:41:42,980
So what happens to them and the rest of the e-waste that comes

603
00:41:42,981 --> 00:41:43,681
here?

604
00:41:44,200 --> 00:41:48,140
We weigh it, we sort it, and it's taken to the dismantling

605
00:41:48,141 --> 00:41:51,280
line where the metals are taken apart, the plastics are taken

606
00:41:51,281 --> 00:41:54,420
out, the circuit boards are taken out, the glass, everything

607
00:41:54,421 --> 00:41:55,560
is just demanufactured.

608
00:41:55,680 --> 00:41:58,920
So think of a manufacturing line, but backwards.

609
00:41:58,921 --> 00:42:06,040
This process is mostly done by hand so as not to contaminate the reusable materials with

610
00:42:06,041 --> 00:42:12,780
the harmful ones, like the lead found in CRT TVs.

611
00:42:12,781 --> 00:42:17,420
Some of the reusable components need to be compressed for easier transport.

612
00:42:17,421 --> 00:42:23,720
Imagine a giant trash compactor, but for plastics and metals.

613
00:42:23,721 --> 00:42:28,780
And these are sent off to processors where they will be melted and be manufactured.

614
00:42:28,781 --> 00:42:31,620
There just may be life after death.

615
00:42:31,621 --> 00:42:35,900
But be careful, because some of that outdated equipment might contain important data.

616
00:42:35,901 --> 00:42:40,500
Because everybody has sensitive information on their computers, whether it's for personal

617
00:42:40,501 --> 00:42:45,060
use or for business use, make sure that when you take it to an e-waste recycler, to ask

618
00:42:45,061 --> 00:42:47,860
them to physically destroy your hard drive.

619
00:42:47,861 --> 00:42:49,700
Deleting it will not do.

620
00:42:49,701 --> 00:42:54,260
As part of the recycling process, we remove the hard drives from the computer, and upon

621
00:42:54,261 --> 00:43:00,260
request we will destroy the hard drives by punching three holes into the disk itself

622
00:43:01,300 --> 00:43:07,060
so that the data is unretrievable but it's not just the information that might leak into the

623
00:43:07,061 --> 00:43:14,100
wrong places the rate of technological change and obsolescence of course has a dark deep and bad

624
00:43:14,101 --> 00:43:20,740
downside some environmentally and socially irresponsible companies export unwanted

625
00:43:20,741 --> 00:43:27,220
electronics overseas, where they are dismantled unsafely, often by children paid pennies a day.

626
00:43:28,920 --> 00:43:33,300
There are a lot of quote-unquote recyclers that will send their electronic waste to

627
00:43:33,301 --> 00:43:38,780
underdeveloped countries where they don't have strict environmental laws and labor laws.

628
00:43:39,240 --> 00:43:43,900
So right now there are villages where people are actually cooking the circuit boards and

629
00:43:43,901 --> 00:43:49,080
inhaling the toxic fumes and then disposing of the waste into their water supply as well as their soil.

630
00:43:50,740 --> 00:43:55,520
However, recyclers like us, we use responsible downstream vendors who have high-tech facilities

631
00:43:55,521 --> 00:43:59,120
to handle these hazardous materials in the proper way.

632
00:44:00,420 --> 00:44:03,420
Today, with technology evolving at near light speed,

633
00:44:04,100 --> 00:44:08,860
don't get too attached to your TVs and cell phones, because their days are numbered.

634
00:44:09,880 --> 00:44:15,180
I'm sure that our laptops and our MP3 players in 10 or 20 years will be collecting dust.

635
00:44:15,460 --> 00:44:17,600
Innovation and change is...

